    "advanced": {
      "title": "Mieux vs Meilleur — Adverb vs Adjective, Agreement & Common Errors",
      "explanation": "'Mieux' is the comparative adverb (better) derived from 'bien' (well). It modifies verbs and describes HOW an action is performed: 'Il chante mieux.' (He sings better.) Adverbs do not agree with gender or number. 'Meilleur(e)(s)' is the comparative adjective (better) derived from 'bon' (good). It modifies nouns and describes the quality of the noun: 'une meilleure idée' (a better idea), 'les meilleurs articles' (the best articles). Adjectives agree with the gender and number of the noun they modify. Common errors: (1) Using 'meilleur' with a verb (incorrect: 'Il chante meilleur'); (2) Using 'mieux' before a noun (incorrect: 'une mieux idée'); (3) Abstract statements with 'meilleur': 'C'est meilleur' (without a noun) is acceptable colloquially but grammatically incomplete. Better: 'C'est mieux' (it is better, adverbial) or 'C'est un meilleur choix' (it is a better choice, with a noun). Superlatives: 'le mieux' (the best, adverbial) vs 'le/la/les meilleur(e)(s)' (the best, adjectival).",
      "examples": [
        {
          "french": "Il chante mieux que moi.",
          "english": "He sings better than I do (adverb with verb)."
        },
        {
          "french": "C'est une meilleure idée.",
          "english": "It is a better idea (adjective with noun)."
        },
        {
          "french": "Ces articles sont les meilleurs.",
          "english": "These articles are the best (plural adjective)."
        },
        {
          "french": "Je me sens mieux aujourd'hui.",
          "english": "I feel better today (adverb with verb)."
        },
        {
          "french": "C'est le mieux pour nous.",
          "english": "It is the best (thing) for us (adverbial superlative)."
        },
        {
          "french": "Elle a la meilleure voix du groupe.",
          "english": "She has the best voice in the group (adjectival superlative)."
        }
      ],
